__d(function(e,i,t,s,o,a,n){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:1,chapter_title:"Integers",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are integers? Write the set of integers.",back:"Integers are all whole numbers including zero and their negatives. The set of integers = {\u2026, \u22124, \u22123, \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, \u2026}. Integers include: positive integers (1, 2, 3, \u2026), negative integers (\u22121, \u22122, \u22123, \u2026), and zero (0). Integers do NOT include fractions (\xbd) or decimals (1.5).",memory_hook:"INT = Integral = complete, whole. No halves, no decimals. Every integer is a step on the number line \u2014 no gaps, no fractions."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the additive inverse of an integer? Give three examples.",back:"The additive inverse of an integer a is \u2212a, such that a + (\u2212a) = 0. Examples: Additive inverse of 7 is \u22127 (since 7 + (\u22127) = 0). Additive inverse of \u221212 is 12 (since \u221212 + 12 = 0). Additive inverse of 0 is 0 (since 0 + 0 = 0). The additive inverse is also called the 'opposite' of an integer.",memory_hook:"Additive Inverse = OPPOSITE + CANCELS to ZERO. Every integer has an exact opposite that destroys it (adds to zero). Like equal and opposite reactions in physics."},{id:"fc03",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the commutative and associative properties of addition of integers with examples.",back:"Commutative Property: a + b = b + a. The order of addition doesn't matter. Example: (\u22125) + 3 = 3 + (\u22125) = \u22122. Associative Property: (a + b) + c = a + (b + c). The grouping doesn't matter. Example: [(\u22123) + 4] + (\u22122) = (\u22123) + [4 + (\u22122)] = \u22121. Both properties help simplify calculations by rearranging or regrouping integers.",memory_hook:"Commutative = Commute (travel/swap). You can swap the order. Associative = Associate (group). You can regroup. Both hold for integer addition and multiplication \u2014 but NOT for subtraction or division."},{id:"fc04",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the rule for multiplying two integers with different signs? Give two examples.",back:"When multiplying two integers with DIFFERENT signs (one positive, one negative), the product is always NEGATIVE. Examples: (+5) \xd7 (\u22123) = \u221215. (\u22128) \xd7 (+4) = \u221232. This rule arises from the pattern of multiplication: 3\xd72=6, 3\xd71=3, 3\xd70=0, 3\xd7(\u22121)=\u22123, 3\xd7(\u22122)=\u22126 \u2014 each step decreases by 3, confirming that positive \xd7 negative = negative.",memory_hook:"DIFFERENT signs = DIFFERENT outcome = NEGATIVE. Like mixing oil and water \u2014 opposites don't combine happily. + and \u2212 together \u2192 always \u2212."},{id:"fc05",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the rule for multiplying two negative integers? Why does negative \xd7 negative = positive?",back:"When multiplying two NEGATIVE integers, the product is always POSITIVE. Example: (\u22124) \xd7 (\u22125) = +20. (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22123) = +9. Pattern reasoning: (\u22123) \xd7 2 = \u22126, (\u22123) \xd7 1 = \u22123, (\u22123) \xd7 0 = 0, (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22121) = +3, (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22122) = +6 \u2014 each step increases by 3, so (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22121) must be +3. The pattern demands it.",memory_hook:"'Enemy of my enemy is my friend.' (\u2212)(\u2212) = (+). Two negatives cancel each other to become positive. Count negative signs: EVEN = positive, ODD = negative."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the distributive property of multiplication over addition for integers, with an example.",back:"Distributive Property: a \xd7 (b + c) = (a \xd7 b) + (a \xd7 c). This lets you 'distribute' multiplication over addition. Example: (\u22123) \xd7 (4 + (\u22122)) = (\u22123) \xd7 4 + (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22122) = \u221212 + 6 = \u22126. Check: (\u22123) \xd7 (4 + (\u22122)) = (\u22123) \xd7 2 = \u22126. \u2713 Also works for subtraction: a \xd7 (b \u2212 c) = ab \u2212 ac.",memory_hook:"Distributive = DISTRIBUTE (share). The multiplier gives its value to EACH term inside the bracket. Like a teacher distributing worksheets to each student \u2014 everyone in the bracket gets multiplied."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the sign rules for division of integers?",back:"Division sign rules (same as multiplication): Positive \xf7 Positive = Positive: (+12) \xf7 (+4) = +3. Negative \xf7 Negative = Positive: (\u221212) \xf7 (\u22124) = +3. Positive \xf7 Negative = Negative: (+12) \xf7 (\u22124) = \u22123. Negative \xf7 Positive = Negative: (\u221212) \xf7 (+4) = \u22123. Rule summary: SAME signs \u2192 Positive quotient. DIFFERENT signs \u2192 Negative quotient.",memory_hook:"Division signs = SAME as multiplication signs. Learn one, learn both. Same signs \u2192 smile (+). Different signs \u2192 frown (\u2212). No new rules to learn for division!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is division by zero undefined? What is 0 \xf7 5?",back:"Division by zero is UNDEFINED because there is no number that, when multiplied by 0, gives a non-zero result. If 6 \xf7 0 = x, then x \xd7 0 = 6 \u2014 but any number times 0 is always 0, never 6. So no such x exists \u2014 the operation is impossible/undefined. However, 0 \xf7 5 = 0, because 0 \xd7 5 = 0. \u2713. Rule: Any number \xf7 0 = UNDEFINED. 0 \xf7 any non-zero number = 0.",memory_hook:"Divide by zero = impossible. Calculators show 'ERROR'. Think: share 6 sweets among 0 people \u2014 it's nonsensical. But share 0 sweets among 5 people = everyone gets 0. Zero \xf7 anything = 0. Anything \xf7 zero = UNDEFINED."},{id:"fc09",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The temperature at a hill station at 6 AM is \u22128\xb0C. It rises by 3\xb0C every hour. What is the temperature at 10 AM?",back:"From 6 AM to 10 AM = 4 hours. Rise in temperature = 4 \xd7 3 = +12\xb0C. Temperature at 10 AM = \u22128 + 12 = 4\xb0C. Answer: 4\xb0C at 10 AM. This uses integer addition (adding a positive to a negative) and multiplication (rate \xd7 time).",memory_hook:"Temperature problems are classic integer word problems. Start value + (change per hour \xd7 number of hours) = final value. Always check: does the answer make sense? Rising from \u22128\xb0C by 12\xb0C gives +4\xb0C \u2014 makes sense!"},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Are integers closed under all four operations? Explain.",back:"Integers are: CLOSED under Addition (sum of two integers is always an integer: \u22123 + 5 = 2 \u2713), CLOSED under Subtraction (difference is always an integer: 2 \u2212 7 = \u22125 \u2713), CLOSED under Multiplication (product is always an integer: (\u22123) \xd7 4 = \u221212 \u2713), NOT CLOSED under Division (quotient may not be an integer: (\u22127) \xf7 2 = \u22123.5 \u2717, not an integer). Closure under division fails whenever the divisor does not exactly divide the dividend.",memory_hook:"Integer closure: +, \u2212, \xd7 = YES (always get integer). \xf7 = NO (may get fraction). Simple test: can (\u22127) \xf7 2 be an integer? No \u2014 it's \u22123.5. Division breaks closure!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"In a quiz, correct = +5, wrong = \u22122, skipped = 0. Priya gets 6 correct, 4 wrong, 5 skipped. Find her score.",back:"Correct answers score = 6 \xd7 (+5) = +30. Wrong answers score = 4 \xd7 (\u22122) = \u22128. Skipped score = 5 \xd7 0 = 0. Total score = 30 + (\u22128) + 0 = 22. Answer: Priya's score is 22. This applies multiplication of integers (positive \xd7 negative = negative) and addition of integers.",memory_hook:"Quiz integer problem: (correct \xd7 +5) + (wrong \xd7 \u22122) + (skipped \xd7 0). Always multiply first, then add. This is a real-life use of integer operations \u2014 competitive exams like JEE, NEET, and UPSC use this scoring system!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the product of: (a) an even number of negative integers? (b) an odd number of negative integers?",back:"(a) Even number of negative integers \u2192 POSITIVE product. Example: (\u22122) \xd7 (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22124) \xd7 (\u22125) = ? \u2192 4 negatives (even) \u2192 positive. |2\xd73\xd74\xd75| = 120. Answer: +120. (b) Odd number of negative integers \u2192 NEGATIVE product. Example: (\u22122) \xd7 (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22124) = ? \u2192 3 negatives (odd) \u2192 negative. |2\xd73\xd74| = 24. Answer: \u221224.",memory_hook:"Count negatives: EVEN = positive (they pair up and cancel). ODD = negative (one is left over unpaired). Like socks: even number of socks = all pairs. Odd number = one leftover = negative sign leftover."},{id:"fc13",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State any three properties of multiplication of integers.",back:"1. Commutative Property: a \xd7 b = b \xd7 a. Example: (\u22123) \xd7 4 = 4 \xd7 (\u22123) = \u221212. 2. Associative Property: (a \xd7 b) \xd7 c = a \xd7 (b \xd7 c). Example: [(\u22122) \xd7 3] \xd7 (\u22124) = (\u22122) \xd7 [3 \xd7 (\u22124)] = 24. 3. Multiplicative Identity: a \xd7 1 = a. Example: (\u22127) \xd7 1 = \u22127. Also: Zero property: a \xd7 0 = 0. Distributive: a(b+c) = ab + ac.",memory_hook:"Multiplication properties = CAD-IZ: Commutative, Associative, Distributive, Identity (\xd71), Zero (\xd70=0). All five matter. The Distributive property is the most useful for mental math."},{id:"fc14",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Use the distributive property to find: (\u221225) \xd7 99",back:"Write 99 = 100 \u2212 1. Then: (\u221225) \xd7 99 = (\u221225) \xd7 (100 \u2212 1) = (\u221225) \xd7 100 \u2212 (\u221225) \xd7 1 = \u22122500 \u2212 (\u221225) = \u22122500 + 25 = \u22122475. Answer: \u22122475. This is much easier than directly multiplying (\u221225) \xd7 99. The distributive property turns hard multiplication into easy steps.",memory_hook:"Distributive property trick: rewrite the number as a sum or difference of a round number. 99 = 100\u22121, 98 = 100\u22122, 51 = 50+1. Then distribute. Mental math becomes fast and accurate."},{id:"fc15",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the absolute value of an integer? Find |\u221215| and |+8|.",back:"The absolute value of an integer is its distance from zero on the number line \u2014 always non-negative. Written as |a|. Rules: If a > 0, |a| = a. If a < 0, |a| = \u2212a (flip the sign). If a = 0, |0| = 0. Examples: |\u221215| = 15 (distance of \u221215 from 0 is 15 steps). |+8| = 8. |0| = 0. Absolute value is used to find the magnitude of an integer regardless of direction.",memory_hook:"Absolute value = ALWAYS POSITIVE (or zero). Strip away the sign, keep the size. |\u221215| = 15 because 15 is 15 steps from zero, whether you go left or right. |a| is like the speed of a car \u2014 speed is always positive, direction is separate."},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A submarine is at \u2212120 m. It rises 45 m, then dives 30 m. What is its final depth?",back:"Start: \u2212120 m. Rise 45 m: \u2212120 + 45 = \u221275 m. Dive 30 m: \u221275 + (\u221230) = \u221275 \u2212 30 = \u2212105 m. Final depth: \u2212105 m (105 metres below sea level). This combines two integer additions \u2014 one positive (rising), one negative (diving).",memory_hook:"Depth problems: BELOW sea level = negative. RISING = add positive. DIVING = add negative (go deeper = more negative). Always draw a quick number line or imagine an elevator going down."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between the commutative property and the associative property?",back:"Commutative Property: concerns the ORDER of operands \u2014 swapping order doesn't change the result. a + b = b + a (addition). a \xd7 b = b \xd7 a (multiplication). Example: 3 + (\u22125) = (\u22125) + 3 = \u22122. Associative Property: concerns the GROUPING (brackets) of operands \u2014 changing grouping doesn't change the result. (a + b) + c = a + (b + c). Example: [(\u22123) + 4] + (\u22122) = (\u22123) + [4 + (\u22122)] = \u22121. Key: Commutative = SWAP. Associative = REGROUP.",memory_hook:"Commutative = Commute = TRAVEL/SWAP positions. Associative = Associate = change your GROUP (who you bracket with). SWAP vs REGROUP \u2014 two different freedoms in addition and multiplication."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Evaluate using properties: (\u22121) \xd7 (\u22122) \xd7 (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22124) \xd7 (\u22125)",back:"Step 1: Count negatives \u2014 5 (ODD number). \u2192 Result is NEGATIVE. Step 2: Multiply absolute values: 1 \xd7 2 \xd7 3 \xd7 4 \xd7 5 = 120. Step 3: Apply sign: answer is \u2212120. Verify step by step: (\u22121)(\u22122) = 2. 2\xd7(\u22123) = \u22126. (\u22126)\xd7(\u22124) = 24. 24\xd7(\u22125) = \u2212120. \u2713 Answer: \u2212120.",memory_hook:"Product of integers: FIRST count negatives (even\u2192+, odd\u2192\u2212), THEN multiply the absolute values. Two-step method. Never lose track of the sign!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A business records a loss of \u20b92,400 per month. What is the total profit/loss over 8 months? What does this mean?",back:"Loss per month = \u2212\u20b92,400. Over 8 months = (\u22122400) \xd7 8 = \u2212\u20b919,200. This means the business has a total LOSS of \u20b919,200 over 8 months. Negative sign represents a loss; positive would represent a profit. If the business then gained \u20b919,200, total = \u221219200 + 19200 = 0 (break-even).",memory_hook:"Business integers: PROFIT = positive, LOSS = negative. Monthly loss \xd7 months = total loss. Real-world integer multiplication that accountants and shopkeepers use every day."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Find the integer that should replace \u2605: (\u221236) \xf7 \u2605 = 4",back:"If (\u221236) \xf7 \u2605 = 4, then \u2605 = (\u221236) \xf7 4 = \u22129. Check: (\u221236) \xf7 (\u22129) = +4. \u2713 (Negative \xf7 negative = positive.) Answer: \u2605 = \u22129. This uses the relationship between division and multiplication: if a \xf7 b = c, then a = b \xd7 c. Also verifies the sign rule: (\u221236) \xf7 (\u22129) = positive.",memory_hook:"Find the missing integer: use the inverse operation. Division \u2194 Multiplication. If a \xf7 b = c \u2192 b = a \xf7 c. Always check your sign \u2014 the sign of the missing integer is determined by the sign rule."}]}},2954,[]);